(Abstract) Tj
T*
(We examined facility-level variation in rehabilitation intensity across eight) Tj
T*
(participating Canadian centers. Mean aerobic exercise time varied from 6 minutes to 18) Tj
T*
(minutes per session across sites \(range 40-64% of participants achieving guideline-) Tj
T*
(recommended minimum intensity levels\). Facilities with lower mean aerobic minutes tended) Tj
T*
(to have larger patient caseloads per therapist and shorter session durations. Session) Tj
T*
(structure analysis revealed that time spent on functional assessment, rest breaks, and) Tj
T*
(patient transportation accounted for 30-45% of scheduled rehabilitation time, leaving) Tj
T*
(55-70% for active therapy. Participants with greater baseline functional capacity and) Tj
T*
(those receiving extended session durations \(>=60 minutes\) achieved higher aerobic minutes.) Tj
T*
(Qualitative observations suggested perceived time constraints and reimbursement) Tj
T*
(limitations as reported barriers to higher-intensity protocols. Facilities that provided) Tj
T*
(real-time biofeedback to therapists \(via displays of heart rate and step data during) Tj
T*
(sessions\) showed non-significantly higher mean aerobic minutes \(13 vs 10 minutes, p =) Tj
T*
(0.11\). These facility and structural factors may represent modifiable barriers to) Tj
T*
(implementing guideline-recommended rehabilitation intensity.) Tj
